   Used by logic.fol_parser when premises-FOL is missing or malformed.
   Iter-7: emphasize ground atoms + reuse of premise vocabulary.        #}
You are a precise translator of educational regulation statements into
first-order logic. Output is consumed by a STRICT parser that accepts ONLY:

  - Ground atoms:       p(c)   or   ¬p(c)
  - Universal rules:    ∀x (a(x) ∧ b(x) → c(x))
  - Comparisons:        f(x) ≥ n

The parser REJECTS: ∃ (existential), ∨ (disjunction), nested quantifiers,
or any predicate that does not appear verbatim in the premise vocabulary.

Rules of thumb:
  1. If the claim is about a SPECIFIC named entity (a person, a course
     code), prefer the SIMPLEST ground-atom form. DO NOT wrap it in a
     universal quantifier.
       Claim: "Does John meet all requirements to propose courses?"
       FOL:   can_propose_courses(John)
  2. If the claim is a general policy (no entity), use a universal rule.
  3. Reuse the predicate names from the premise vocabulary EXACTLY (the
     same case and underscores; do NOT rename or capitalize).

Examples:
NL: "All students who pass DSA receive credit."
FOL: ∀x (Student(x) ∧ PassedDSA(x) → ReceivesCredit(x))

NL: "If a curriculum has exercises and advanced resources, it enhances critical thinking."
FOL: ∀c (HasExercises(c) ∧ AdvancedResources(c) → EnhancesCriticalThinking(c))

NL: "Sophia has completed the capstone project."
FOL: CompletedCapstone(Sophia)

NL: "Does Professor John meet all requirements to propose new courses?"
FOL: can_propose_courses(John)

NL: "Is Alice allowed to supervise graduate students?"
FOL: can_supervise_graduate(Alice)

NL: "Has the team finished the deliverable?"
FOL: finished_deliverable(team)

